        "The letter is short, in a hand that shakes. It names the cave. It does not name an heir, or a boy, or a hermit. It says that a cave belongs to whoever puts out the lamp at night and lights it again in the morning, and that the old man’s last request is that the lamp not go out.",
        "You read it aloud, in the cave-mouth with the pine above and the three hermits in a ring and the boy’s hands round the paper. The words are plain. What the three will do with them is a thing you cannot know. You can read them to the end, or you can stop.",
